<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The template the bundle's `Contents/Info.plist` is generated from.

  The two version fields below are the only substitution in this file, and a
  template asking for any other placeholder is refused rather than shipped with
  it unfilled. They are filled at bundle time from `[workspace.package] version`
  in the workspace `Cargo.toml` — the same single line
  `bt_app::version::tests::the_version_is_the_manifests_and_nothing_elses` holds
  the executable, the PE resource and every diagnostic header to, and its
  sibling `the_plist_carries_the_workspace_version_twice_and_no_literal` renders
  this file and holds both fields to. This file therefore carries no literal
  version, and copying one in here by hand is the thing those gates exist to
  catch.

  The placeholder is deliberately not quoted in this comment: it would be filled
  in here too, and the generated plist would carry a sentence that had turned
  into a statement about a version number.

  `cargo run -q -p bt-winres --bin render-info-plist` prints what this renders
  to; `crates/bt-winres/src/plist.rs` is the renderer.

  This template is not signed and not installed; the generated plist is.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>

	<!-- APPL is what makes this directory an application rather than a folder
	     with an executable in it; Launch Services reads it before anything
	     else here. -->
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>

	<!-- Permanent. TCC keys every permission the user ever grants on this
	     string, so changing it after a release throws away the grants that
	     release collected. Reverse DNS under the project's GitHub home. -->
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>io.github.lulu-loopp.folio</string>

	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Folio</string>

	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Folio</string>

	<!-- `Contents/MacOS/folio`. The same file name the Windows build gives
	     `OriginalFilename`, minus the extension. -->
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>folio</string>

	<!-- Both version fields are the manifest's, and both are filled from the
	     one line. `CFBundleShortVersionString` is what the user is shown;
	     `CFBundleVersion` is what the system compares builds with, and it
	     accepts only dotted integers — a release channel suffix belongs to the
	     tag and never reaches the manifest, so neither field ever sees one. -->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>@VERSION@</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>@VERSION@</string>

	<!-- `Contents/Resources/Folio.icns`, named without its extension as the
	     key wants it. -->
	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>Folio</string>

	<!-- The deployment target, stated rather than inherited from whichever
	     macOS the build machine happens to be running. -->
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>14.0</string>

	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.developer-tools</string>

	<!-- The window is drawn at the display's backing scale. Without this the
	     system hands the process a 1x framebuffer and magnifies it, which for
	     a terminal is the whole product. -->
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- A text window has no business holding the discrete GPU awake on a
	     machine that has one. -->
	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticGraphicsSwitching</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Word for word the notice `LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE` carry, and
	     the same string `crates/bt-app/build.rs` writes into `LegalCopyright`
	     on Windows. One notice, two platforms. -->
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Copyright (c) 2026 Weiyi Shi and Folio contributors. Licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.</string>

	<!--
	  **No usage-description keys, on purpose.** The plan names exactly two
	  permissions Folio asks for (§5 ④): Notifications, and Accessibility for
	  the global shortcut. Neither is a plist-declared purpose string —
	  `UNUserNotificationCenter` prompts against the bundle identifier and the
	  code signature, and Accessibility is granted in System Settings against
	  the same signature, with no `Info.plist` key to carry a sentence. Adding a
	  `NS…UsageDescription` here for either would be a permission this product
	  does not request. Nothing else in §2 or §6 asks the user for anything.

	  `NSServices` is below, and the sentence this note used to end on was that
	  it would be: a Service needs a registered provider object as well as the
	  array (§8 Q3), and M4-9 built the object.
	-->

	<!--
	  **Finder's *Services ▸ Open in Folio*** (M4-9, plan §8 Q3 ruled
	  2026-09-12: `NSServices` only, no Finder Sync extension).

	  This array is half of the feature and the other half is
	  `crates/bt-platform/src/macos_services.rs`. AppKit reads the array to
	  decide whether to draw the row; when the row is pressed it sends
	  `<NSMessage>:userData:error:` to the object that file registers with
	  `-[NSApplication setServicesProvider:]`. Either half alone is a row that
	  does nothing or an object nobody calls, which is why
	  `the_service_this_bundle_declares_is_the_one_the_provider_answers` reads
	  both files and asserts the two spellings are one.

	  `NSMessage` is the **first word of the selector and not the whole of it**:
	  AppKit appends `:userData:error:` itself. Writing the full selector here
	  is the plausible mistake, and it is a row that draws, is pressed, and
	  reports a failure to the reader.

	  `NSPortName` is `CFBundleName` above. `NSSendTypes` is one type, the one
	  Finder writes a selection as; the provider reads the pasteboard by class
	  (`NSURL`), so a sender old enough to write `NSFilenamesPboardType`
	  instead is still understood — but it is not advertised, because this row
	  is for Finder and Finder does not write it.

	  **No `NSRequiredContext`.** The row is wanted for files and for folders
	  alike, and any `NSServiceCategory` or `NSTextContent` restriction here
	  would take it off one of them.

	  **The item's words are English and there is no localized name**, which is
	  a gap with a reason rather than an oversight: `在 Folio 中打开` exists in
	  the string table as `Text::ExplorerCommandVerb`'s Chinese side and is what
	  Explorer's row says on the other platform, but a localized Services item
	  is a `Resources/<lang>.lproj/` in the bundle, and this bundle has no
	  localized resources at all — `CFBundleDevelopmentRegion` is not set and
	  nothing else in it is translated. One localized string in an otherwise
	  unlocalized bundle would be a Chinese row in an English menu bar. Bundle
	  localization is its own ticket; this row joins it when it happens.
	-->
	<key>NSServices</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSMenuItem</key>
			<dict>
				<key>default</key>
				<string>Open in Folio</string>
			</dict>
			<key>NSMessage</key>
			<string>openInFolio</string>
			<key>NSPortName</key>
			<string>Folio</string>
			<key>NSSendTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.file-url</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
